  "_comment": "Cross-sections run from the centre outwards to the nearside for ONE direction of travel; two-way types mirror it. Dual types are drawn as one stroke and placed as two one-way carriageways median_gap_m apart. Speeds are stored in the region's display unit (mph); the core converts to m/s.",
  "_sources": {
    "vertical_k": "DMRB CD 109 Table 3.1 (desirable min crest K / sag K by design speed). Recalled values, not yet checked against the current CD 109; starting points only, tuned by eye.",
    "min_radius": "DMRB CD 109 desirable minimum horizontal radius by design speed; residential uses a Manual for Streets-style tighter value. Advisory only.",
    "widths": "Typical UK practice; tuned by eye.",
    "rural_types": "estimate: a_road_rural (2 x 3.65 m, 1 m hard strips, 2.5 m verges, 60 mph), b_road_rural (2 x 3.2 m, no hard strip, 1.5 m verges), country_lane (3.5 m total, see note) and main_street (3.25 m lanes, 2 m parking bays, 2.5 m footways) are plausible UK practice, not checked against DMRB / Manual for Streets; the rural types are kerbless. The lane model has no single shared two-way lane, so country_lane is the narrowest valid two-lane type: two 1.75 m half lanes, one per direction, which together read as one 3.5 m surface; it has no marking set (markings empty).",
    "street_lights": "estimate: spacing, setbacks, column heights, outreach and arrangement are plausible UK practice (BS 5489 style values recalled, not checked); the motorway is unlit by default (rural motorways are usually unlit); tuned by eye."
  },
